  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonably warm temperatures and comfortable \nhumidity through Wednesday. Mainly dry outside of isolated \nshowers/storms in NE NC. \n\nMostly clear early this morning with a bit of chill in the air, \nespecially away from the coast. Already seeing a few obs in the \nupper 50s in a few of our rural Piedmont sites with 60s elsewhere \n(70s near the coast). While another warm day is expected today \n(highs mid-upper 80s), dew points in the 50s (60s near the coast) \nshould make it feel quite comfortable. High pressure remains in \nplace Tuesday and then gradually shifts offshore Wednesday, which \nwill then allow for a gradual return to the humidity. However, \nTuesday should be another with dew points in the 50s and it will \nagain feel quite nice out despite the warm temps. \n\nExpecting mainly dry wx for most of our CWA through Wednesday. CAMs \ncontinue to indicate some isolated showers could develop near the \nAlbemarle Sound (and points S) this afternoon, but the coverage \ndoesn't justify more than a 20-30% PoP. Instability also looks on \nthe meager side so don't expect more than an isolated rumble of \nthunder with any activity. Could be a similar scenario Tuesday and \nWednesday as a weak sfc trough remains in place along the NC/SC \ncoast. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Unsettled conditions return mid to late week with \ndaily chances for showers/storms. \n\nMoisture returns to the Mid-Atlantic later Wednesday through the end \nof the week with the offshore high and SW flow aloft. This favors a \nreturn to daily chances for showers and storms. As of now, Thursday \nand Friday look like the days with the highest coverage of storms as \na frontal system approaches the area. Some stronger storms could \nalso be possible, depending on the strength of the flow aloft. Zonal \nflow sets up by next weekend with continuing, but probably lower, \nshower/storm chances.",
